		{
			dependencies: [
				"74469710D910C19D"
				"53EC3831D66769CA"
			]
			description: [
				"Now that you have &6HSS-G&r, you can begin automating &bExtended Crafting&r recipes in an easier way than before. "
				""
				"To begin, you'll need to craft one of each extended &3Package Crafter&r listed above. Put an &3Unpackager&r on top of each one, and then a &3Packager&r anywhere you want. These are all AE2 devices, so you'll need to connect them to an ME Network."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Next, right-click the &3Package Recipe Encoder&r, and you should see a button saying \"Processing\": this button cycles through recipe types, including the various Extended Crafting tables. You'll have to choose the appropriate table for the recipe you want to encode."
				""
				"&6Holders&r must be encoded in pairs: one for the Packager, and one for the Unpackager. Select the appropriate table for the recipe, look up the recipe you want to encode, and press &6[+]&r in &bEMI&r to fill it in. Put two Holders in the top-left slot and press Save to encode the recipe to both of them. Finally, put each Holder into the respective machines."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"If you've done everything correctly, you should see the item appear as craftable in a Terminal."
				""
				"While it the crafting process is &3significantly slower than using &6Auto Crafting Tables&r, &ePackagedAuto is able to easily parallelize&r. You can surround an Unpackager with Crafters and it will use all of them. It's also possible to add more Unpackagers with Crafters to keep up with the Packager, as long as the Holders are all the same."
				""
				"If this is too confusing, try out"
				"[\"\",{\"underlined\":true,\"text\":\"Auto Crafting Tables\",\"clickEvent\":{\"action\":\"change_page\",\"value\":\"0DB8063A7972E1C0\"}}]"
			]
			icon: "packagedexcrafting:ultimate_crafter"
			id: "70D9B3C6539720DE"
			optional: true
			rewards: [{
				id: "083A8F49762A37FE"
				item: "kubejs:moni_quarter"
				type: "item"
			}]
			shape: "hexagon"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "6CEACB6992E7F9D9"
					item: "packagedexcrafting:advanced_crafter"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "0F4C64194342AA5E"
					item: "packagedexcrafting:elite_crafter"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "55E2CB3E78077651"
					item: "packagedexcrafting:ultimate_crafter"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "702A5745B9568FA0"
					item: "packagedauto:unpackager"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "2648C42313B6B935"
					item: "packagedauto:packager"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "028285C508794CDC"
					item: "packagedauto:recipe_holder"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&9Automating Extended Crafting"
			x: -1.25d
			y: 2.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["0EF85170074E70B3"]
			description: [
				"You can easily automate most &bGregTech&r machines with &aME Pattern Providers&r and processing &aPatterns&r early on by using the machines' auto-output feature."
				""
				"Use &9the Machine's GUI&r to relocate the output side of a machine where you want it to be, then press the \"&3Auto Output&r\" and \"&3Allow Input From Output Side&r\" buttons, and put an ME Pattern Provider on that side of the machine."
				""
				"You can also allow input from the output side by shift-right-clicking with a screwdriver on the output side via the machine grid."
				""
				"Right-click the machine and enable item auto-output (&6orange box&r in the bottom-left corner of the GUI)."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Now, stuff the &6ME Pattern Provider&r with processing patterns. The next time you order an item, the ME Pattern Provider will push ingredients into the machine, and the machine will push outputs back into the Pattern Provider once they're ready, completing the craft. No need to use nodes or import buses!"
				""
				"Furthermore, you can use &3Blocking Mode&r, which ensures that no items are in the inventory before placing the items in, which is a handy way to prevent conflicts. &2Even better, blocking mode ignores Programmable Circuits and Molds!&r\n&2Please note that non-consumable items should stay in the machine, and not be included in the pattern made. Yes, this means you should make a separate machine for each non-consumable, at least until you get &3Large Machines&2.&r"
			]
			icon: "minecraft:book"
			id: "6E27B9938B280291"
			shape: "hexagon"
			size: 1.25d
			tasks: [{
				id: "363E7C6EC336B44C"
				title: "Simple GregTech Automation"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			x: 2.0d
			y: 7.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["6F32A8739D8E792A"]
			description: [
				"Handling &6Patterns&r gets tedious pretty quickly, from not having enough slots to move tens of them at a time, to having to multiply them when upgrading your &3Large Machines&r, which, when equipped with &3Parallel Control Hatches&r, may require you to supply more than one item at a time to utilize them to their fullest."
				""
				"You don't want to supply one ingot and get two wires when your &3Large Wire Factory&r can take &e64 ingots&r and make &e128 wires&r at a time, right?"
				""
				"That's where the &6Pattern Modifier&r comes into play, which makes it possible to &emove, multiply, divide and clear patterns in bulk&r. "
				""
				"&cKeep in mind: The pattern multiplier function only works on &oProcessing Patterns.&r"
			]
			id: "73908E06B75B9C9E"
			rewards: [{
